    "headline": "SCATTERED spends 59-60% of every budget on COORDINATES rather than values, and still only wins at the largest budget. COLUMN spends 0.1% on index and wins at the smallest. ROW is nearly worthless (+2.8% to +8.7%), which is a structural fact rather than a tuning failure: quantization error hurts in proportion to per-INPUT-CHANNEL activation energy, so corrections aligned with columns match the weighting and corrections aligned with output rows do not.",
    "the_actual_answer": "The best correction topology is the one that carries NO index at all. A single function-fitted binary PLANE at 1.25 b/elem beats every index-carrying topology at 1.0 b/elem on every site (57.8% mean reduction against COLUMN's 30.9% and SCATTERED's 30.4% across all budgets), and unlike them it already has a kernel measured UNDER the decode budget (binary_planes_k2, 0.6647 ps/element, -17.9%).",
    "kernel_cost_half": "The verify demands the winner be shown against its KERNEL cost, and this is where SCATTERED dies rather than merely loses. Its only kernel in the tree, strand_outlier_correct, performs one atomic_fetch_add per outlier into the output row, so every entry sharing a row serialises. At the 1.0 b/elem budget that is 235,929 atomics on a 1536-row SLICE -- of one tensor, of 401. A topology that wins on paper and thrashes on device is a NET-LOSS under G044, and this one is not even bound: 0 references from qwen38_hybrid_decode.rs."
